MARKETING AND SCOPE COPY — CANONICAL SOURCE FOR EVERY
PRODUCT

\================================================================================



Principle

\- This standard is the parent source for
customer-facing philosophy, audience, and honest scope copy — not a separate
marketing playbook that can drift.

\- Product-specific wording lives in
reference/MARKETING\_COPY.txt (or {Product}/MARKETING\_COPY.txt when products
split repos) — derived from sections in this file, then adapted to the
product’s one job.

\- In-app About, upgrade panels, Help summaries, store
listings, and web pages pull from the same will/won’t lists — updocs keeps them
aligned.



What to derive from this standard (mapping)

\- Through-line + Brand Philosophy stamp → philosophy
tagline, hub hero, About, and philosophy-banner.

\- Brand Philosophy principles → support local-first
callouts; must stay aligned with nivo78.com/about/.

\- One job, done well + Handoff honesty + What a good
Nivo78 app will do → “What {Product} will do for you”

  bullets
(product-specific tasks + validate + hand off).

\- Expectation honesty + Transparent → will/won’t
lists, Help limits, and About copy that prevent

  post-install
surprise (capabilities and boundaries together).

\- Local-first and trustworthy data → philosophy-banner
and local-first callout on support and product pages.

\- What a good Nivo78 app will not do + Brand
Philosophy avoid-list + Handoff honesty limits → family-wide

  “won’t do”
bullets (no cloud login, no silent upload, no nags, no trackers, etc.).

\- Commercial honesty + Fair Business Model → tier
lines, subscription justification, good-fit / skip-if, device restrictions.

\- Trust sections (v1.3+) → security summaries on
support; don’t duplicate full vault spec on marketing pages.



Per-product marketing copy file (required for shipped
products)

\- Maintain reference/MARKETING\_COPY.txt (APA:
reference/MARKETING\_COPY.txt) with:

  - Core pitch
(short + long)

  - What
{Product} will do for you (bullets — shipped behavior only)

  - What
{Product} won’t do for you (bullets — permanent boundaries; say “won’t,” not
“not yet”)

  - Good fit if
you / Skip if (optional but recommended)

  - FAQ snippets
aligned with web and in-app copy

  - Pointers to
engine copy constants and hosted website PHP when applicable

\- When standard or shipped behavior changes, update
MARKETING\_COPY.txt first, then web HTML, in-app registries, and Help KB via updocs.



Voice rules (marketing derived from standard)

\- Plain language for everyday users — no pitch-deck
superlatives unless literally true.

\- Will-do bullets describe actions the app performs
today; won’t-do bullets describe boundaries the code and policy enforce.

\- Aim every webpage and Help sentence at trust: if a
limit would disappoint a reasonable buyer,

  put it in
won’t-do (or adjacent plain language) before they purchase or depend on the
feature.

\- No “coming soon,” “planned,” or roadmap language — deferred
capability belongs in “won’t do,” not “will do later.”

\- Good-fit / skip-if describe audience and situation —
not insults; skip-if is optional when good-fit is enough.





